'en_content'=><<<'HTML'
<p>When frying potatoes, croquettes, fish or coated foods, the oil can suddenly fill with bubbles and develop a layer of foam on the surface. Sometimes this is a temporary effect caused by the food itself. In other cases it is a clue that the oil contains many residues or has been through too many heating cycles.</p>
<p>The useful question is not simply “is foam bad?” but <strong>what is creating it, when it appears and how the oil behaves before and after frying</strong>. Those details separate normal frying activity from oil that should be replaced.</p>
<h2>Bubbles and persistent foam are not quite the same</h2>
<p>As soon as moist food enters hot oil, some of its water rapidly turns into steam. The steam rises and creates bubbles. This is normal frying behaviour and is particularly vigorous with foods that contain a lot of water.</p>
<p><strong>Persistent foam</strong> is different. Many small bubbles become stabilised and form a layer that does not collapse quickly. Starch, proteins, loose flour, crumbs and compounds produced as frying oil ages can all help stabilise those bubbles.</p>
<h2>1. Food moisture: the immediate cause</h2>
<p>Water is why food sizzles when it enters hot oil. The wetter its surface, the stronger the initial release of steam. Freshly washed potatoes, vegetables, wet fish or thawed food that has not been dried can produce much more bubbling.</p>
<p>For safety, <strong>never add water directly to a pan or fryer containing hot oil</strong>. Rapid vaporisation can throw hot oil out of the vessel. Drying food thoroughly reduces splattering and limits the temperature drop when the food is added.</p>
<h2>2. Starch from potatoes and other foods</h2>
<p>Cut or grated potatoes can release surface starch. Some of it enters the oil and can help produce more stable foam. Flour and starch used in coatings can have a similar effect.</p>
<p>When a recipe permits rinsing cut potatoes to remove surface starch, do so before cooking and then dry them completely. Putting rinsed potatoes into oil while they are still wet defeats the purpose and creates a much greater splashing risk.</p>
<h2>3. Flour, breadcrumbs and loose coating</h2>
<p>During batch frying, every croquette or breaded piece can shed particles. Those fragments continue cooking after the food is removed. Eventually they darken, burn and change the oil.</p>
<p>Filtering cold oil between uses can remove solids, but it cannot reverse chemical changes already caused by heat. Oil that shows clear deterioration does not become fresh again merely because crumbs have been filtered out.</p>
<h2>4. Overloading the pan and lowering the temperature</h2>
<p>Adding too much food at once causes the oil temperature to fall. The food keeps releasing water while the oil takes longer to recover. Frying becomes slower and wetter, with more bubbles and residues accumulating in the pan.</p>
<p>Moderate batches help maintain a stable working temperature. The International Olive Council uses about <strong>180°C</strong> as a common ideal frying reference and notes that olive oil performs well under heat because of its high oleic-acid content and natural antioxidants.</p>
<h2>5. Heavily reused oil can foam more</h2>
<p>Repeated heating promotes oxidation, hydrolysis and other changes. Each frying session also adds moisture and food residues. As these effects accumulate, oil can darken, thicken, develop unpleasant aromas, smoke at a lower temperature and produce more persistent foam.</p>
<p><strong>There is no single domestic reuse count that guarantees oil is still good.</strong> Temperature, heating time, food type, residue load and storage between uses all matter. The International Olive Council gives a general recommendation not to reuse olive oil more than four or five times, provided it has been handled correctly and shows no signs of deterioration.</p>
<h2>Does a little foam mean the oil must be discarded?</h2>
<p>Not necessarily. Foam that appears when wet potatoes or coated food is added and then subsides as steam production falls can be driven mostly by the food. By contrast, if <strong>oil foams heavily with little food, the foam remains, the oil smells unpleasant, is very dark or thick, or starts smoking earlier than it used to</strong>, replacing it is sensible.</p>
<p>Do not try to rescue clearly degraded oil by topping it up with fresh oil. The fresh portion dilutes what is already present, but it does not remove degradation products or restore the used oil to its original condition.</p>
<h2>Can extra virgin olive oil foam?</h2>
<p>Yes. EVOO can receive moisture, flour, starch and proteins from food just like any other cooking oil. What differs is its composition: a high proportion of oleic acid and natural antioxidants contributes to good heat stability.</p>
<p>That does not make it indestructible. Overheating it, leaving it empty over high heat or repeatedly frying food full of residues will still reduce its quality. For a broader comparison, read our guide to <a href="/en/olive-oil-or-sunflower-oil-for-frying-which-to-choose/">olive oil versus sunflower oil for frying</a>.</p>
<h2>How to reduce foaming at home</h2>
<ul>
<li>Dry food thoroughly before it goes into the oil.</li>
<li>Shake off loose flour or breadcrumbs.</li>
<li>Do not overcrowd the pan or fryer.</li>
<li>Avoid overheating and use an appropriate temperature for the food.</li>
<li>Remove loose solids between batches when practical.</li>
<li>If reusing oil, let it cool, filter when appropriate and store it away from light, air and heat.</li>
<li>Do not blend clearly degraded oil with fresh oil simply to extend its life.</li>
</ul>
<h2>When should frying oil be replaced?</h2>
<p>Judge the complete picture rather than one isolated sign. Replace oil when it develops <strong>abnormally persistent foam, early smoking, rancid or unpleasant odours, marked thickening, severe darkening or a heavy load of burnt residues</strong>. Oil that has obviously overheated should also be discarded.</p>
<p>Controlled frying begins with appropriate oil, enough oil for the food, dry ingredients and stable temperature. Under those conditions foam becomes easier to interpret: often it is simply steam and food particles; when it persists together with other changes, it is a useful sign that the oil has been pushed too far.</p>
